description:
Lesson 2.01 protects the device with an SDDL string such as `D:P(A;;GA;;;SY)(A;;GA;;;BA)`. Write a small version of the check Windows makes when someone opens it:

```cpp
struct Ace { bool allow; std::uint32_t rights; std::string trustee; };

bool parseDacl(const std::string& sddl, std::vector<Ace>& out);
bool accessCheck(const std::vector<Ace>& dacl, const std::vector<std::string>& groups, std::uint32_t desired);
```

**parseDacl** reads `D:` optionally followed by flags (`P`, `AI`; ignored), then ACEs in parentheses: `(type;flags;rights;;;trustee)` with exactly six fields. The type is `A` (allow) or `D` (deny); flags are ignored; rights are `GA` (all: `0x10000000`), `GR` (read: `0x80000000`), `GW` (write: `0x40000000`), `GX` (execute: `0x20000000`), any concatenation of them (`GRGW`), or a hex number `0x...`; the trustee is a two-letter alias such as `SY`, `BA`, `WD`, `AU`, `IU`. Anything else fails.

**accessCheck**: the caller belongs to `groups` (aliases). Expand `GA` in each ACE to `GR | GW | GX` first. Walk the ACEs **in order**, considering only those whose trustee is one of the caller's groups: a deny ACE that covers any desired right that is not yet granted denies access at once; an allow ACE grants its rights. Access is allowed when every desired right has been granted. An empty DACL allows nothing.

